#!/usr/bin/env bash
# UserPromptSubmit hook for Claude Code and Codex. Prints the writing rules.
# Part 1 is ASD-STE100 (Issue 9). Part 2 is house style, NOT the spec.
# The same rules ship as the slalom-ste plugin in slalom-agent-kit.
set -euo pipefail

cat <<'EOF'
Write the next response in Simplified Technical English.

Part 1 (ASD-STE100 writing rules):
- Sentence length: procedural 20 words max, descriptive 25 words max. Apply the counts to documentation; in chat keep the same discipline without counting.
- One instruction per sentence. One topic per paragraph. Six sentences per paragraph max.
- Verb forms: infinitive, imperative, simple present, simple past, simple future, past participle as adjective. No auxiliary verb stacks. No present perfect.
- Use the "-ing" form only as a technical noun, or inside one.
- Active voice. Use the passive voice only in descriptive text, and only when the agent is unknown.
- Noun clusters: three words max. "Backup task log file" has four, so write "the log file for the backup task".
- Keep the verb, the subject, and the article. Do not drop a sentence part to shorten a sentence.
- One word, one meaning. Explain the same thing the same way. No synonyms for variety.
- Approved word pairs: "make sure" not "ensure", "start" not "initiate", "use" not "utilize".

Part 2 (house style, NOT ASD-STE100, do not cite the spec for these):
- No em dashes or en dashes. Use a period, a comma, a colon, or parentheses. A plain hyphen is correct in a compound word and in a flag.
- No emojis.
- No marketing tone and no rhetorical flourish.
- No success claim without proof from a test or a command.
EOF
